/local-offload
/local-offload.exe
local-offload.exe~
/.run/
/.testrun/
/.testrun2/
*.db

# machine-specific working configs — use config.example.json as the template
config.json
config-*.json
config.json.bak*
config.json.*.bak
test-config.json
# ...but the installer's shipped config template IS tracked
!setup/templates/config.json

# setup.sh-generated artifacts (written into the harness source dir)
/offload-family.llama-swap.yaml
/serve-offload.sh

# local TTS venv (Chatterbox) — not tracked
.tts-venv/

# prep/utility venv + binary rollback backups — never track (LO-14: two ~14MB
# exe backups were tracked; the broader patterns catch every rename variant)
/.prep-venv/
*.old.exe
local-offload.exe.bak-*
*.exe.bak-*
*.exe.old-*

# scratch render/probe artifacts
_tmp-*.svg

# subagent-driven-development scratch
.superpowers/
local-agent.exe
bin/local-agent-linux

# built binaries under bin/ — never track (0.16.0 release briefly swept them in via add -A)
/bin/

# fleet-serve stdio redirects (local runtime artifact, never committed)
fleet-serve.log
fleet-serve.log.out

# Printed CLIs under tools/ (docs/systems/printed-clis.md) are separate Go modules
# with their own build outputs. `/bin/` above is anchored to the repo root, so it
# does NOT cover them — a printed CLI's Makefile writes bin/<name>[.exe] inside its
# own module, and the printing-press writes release bundles (.mcpb, staged trees)
# to build/. Neither is source; both are tens of MB.
tools/*/bin/
tools/*/build/
# Run reports the printing-press drops next to the module on every reprint. They
# are evidence from one machine's run, not the artifact we vendor.
tools/*/dogfood-results.json
tools/*/workflow-verify-report.json
tools/*/apify-actor-audit-report.json
tools/*/.printing-press-*-polish.json
# `llamaswap-pp-cli captures export` writes this into the module dir by default. It is
# a live capture of real request/response bodies from whatever server was running —
# runtime output and a data-leak hazard in a public repo, never source.
tools/*/captures.jsonl

# ...but re-include a printed CLI's OWN config files. The `config.json` / `config-*.json`
# patterns near the top of this file are unanchored — they target the harness's
# machine-specific working configs, and they also match at any depth, including inside a
# vendored module where such a file is SOURCE. That silently dropped
# tools/llamaswap/internal/lsconfig/schema/config-schema.json, which validate.go pulls in
# with //go:embed: the module then does not compile at all, and the omission is invisible
# in a diff. Negations must come after the pattern they undo.
!tools/**/config.json
!tools/**/config-*.json

# integrations/* are per-agent integration paths with their own package managers
integrations/**/node_modules/
integrations/**/bun.lock
